Fix a soundness issue in the allocation of function values: a function value counted as allocated in any heap in which its reads frame was allocated, though it may capture an object it does not read (`() => n` reads nothing and returns `n`), and allocation is monotone along heap succession, so a function value could be allocated before an object it captures or its reads frame grows to, which proved false. A function value is now allocated where what it captures is: a lambda where its free variables, `this` and the heaps it uses are, and a function handle where its receiver and, for a two-state function, its previous heap are. Its reads frame and results are allocated there, as before. A general arrow type `A ~> B` now counts as a type that may contain references, whatever `A` and `B` are, since its values show captured references through their reads frames: it no longer satisfies `(!new)`, a datatype holding one is no longer allocated in every heap, and a lambda that captures one is allocated only where it is. Quantifiers over general arrows still range over all their values. Partial (`-->`) and total (`->`) arrows read nothing and are unchanged.

Keep the existing bounded-polymorphism compiler regression registered with the allocation controls: a generic constant-field lambda must be instantiated at the selected receiver's actual type arguments. The shipped constant-field substitution already provides this map and is unchanged.
